  "worked_solution": "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-5)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-5t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(5)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(5)^{2}-5(5)+C \\\\ 0 & =25-25+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the train at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}-5t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the train is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}-5t=t(t-5)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14094076/assets/afe458cdaf5eedbfb78cd9067b0c8caa209535391156bb5de14de34562f3cc1c.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever  $0\\,<\\,t<5.$",
